<h1 class="article-title">Karosta Canal Remediation Project Phase II Begins</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority is beginning implementation of the project “Remediation of the Historically Contaminated Site in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja, Phase II”. Its objective is to reduce the negative impact of historically contaminated sediments in Karosta Canal on the environment, human health and the ecological status of the Baltic Sea. Remediation will involve removing 202,247 m³ of sediment from the canal bed that does not meet statutory standards and contains 400 tonnes of hazardous chemicals. The project is financed under the Norwegian Financial Mechanism 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment”. Total costs amount to EUR 5,117,647.05, of which EUR 4,350,000.00 is Norwegian Financial Mechanism funding.</p><p>The project will benefit society as a whole by contributing to the objectives and actions of the Helsinki Commission (HELCOM) Baltic Sea Action Plan: restoring the good ecological status of the Baltic Sea, ensuring the diversity and dynamics of its ecosystem, and maintaining a clean, healthy and productive marine environment under conditions typical of the sea. The marine environment should be used sustainably, preserving its potential for current and future generations. As a result of the project, Liepāja residents will no longer face the risk of exposure to historical pollution, and the ecological status of the sea and the quality of the surrounding environment will be significantly improved.</p><p>According to Liepāja SEZ CEO Jānis Lapiņš, the project is unique and will achieve several goals: addressing environmental and ecological issues in the Baltic Sea region, removing the site from HELCOM’s Baltic Sea list of “hot spots”, and creating opportunities to further develop active business operations in the Karosta Canal basin and adjacent areas.</p><p>The project is being implemented with the Latvian partner, the association “Baltijas krasti”, and the Norwegian partner, the Norwegian Institute for Water Research. The partners’ experience is essential to achieving the project objectives. Knowledge transfer is needed for successful implementation, along with public information activities about the impact of climate change on historically contaminated sites and the interaction between such sites, human health and the ecological status of the sea.</p><p>On 13 September 2021, the Liepāja SEZ Authority signed an agreement with the Latvian project partner, the association “Baltijas krasti”. Following coordination with the Ministry of Environmental Protection and Regional Development, an agreement with the Norwegian Institute for Water Research is expected to be signed within the next month. The Authority is simultaneously preparing procurement documentation to identify the most economically advantageous bid for the works. The project must be completed by 31 December 2023, although most of the contaminated soil is expected to be dredged during the 2022 construction season.</p><p> </p><p>History:</p><p>Karosta Canal (cadastral No. 1700 010 0073) is an artificial waterway built in the late nineteenth century for the naval fleet of the Russian Empire. From the second half of the twentieth century, the area was a closed military zone of the naval forces of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR) for approximately 50 years, until their withdrawal from Latvia in 1994. During the Soviet period, the canal was operated without regard for potential environmental harm: ships were refuelled improperly and wastewater was discharged into the canal, with heavy-metal and petroleum contamination far exceeding permissible limits. Toxic substances from these practices accumulated in the canal-bed sediments, creating a serious ecological threat to the Baltic Sea. After the collapse of the USSR and the military withdrawal in 1994, there was no longer a responsible polluter to whom the “polluter pays” principle under the Law on Pollution could be applied. As early as 1992, HELCOM identified Karosta in Liepāja as one of the most polluted military sites in the Baltic Sea region, and the remediation of Karosta Canal was included in the HELCOM Baltic Sea Action Plan. The canal is registered in the Register of Polluted and Potentially Polluted Sites under No. 17004/5138 and appears on HELCOM’s Baltic Sea hot-spot list as Hot Spot No. 48, Liepāja City and Port.</p><p>Following the collapse of the USSR, the Liepāja SEZ Authority became the manager of the historically contaminated site. The state entrusted it with Karosta Canal (cadastral No. 1700 010 0073) for port operations and with the special sediment disposal site built at the eastern end of the canal in 1998 with PHARE financial support (cadastral No. 1700 010 0306) for managing contaminated sediments.</p><p>Between 1994 and 2010, the Liepāja SEZ Authority and state institutions financed dozens of geoecological studies to prepare the Karosta Canal remediation project. The decision to implement Phase I was not made until 2012. With EU Cohesion Fund financing, the project “Remediation of the Historically Contaminated Site in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja, Phase I” was completed by 31 December 2015. Large-scale man-made waste (scrap metal, concrete blocks, timber and other materials) was removed and disposed of from a 78-hectare part of the canal. Approximately 60,000 m³ of contaminated sediments were also removed from the most polluted 10-hectare section and placed in a specially equipped disposal site at the eastern end of Karosta Canal.</p><p> </p><p>About the project partners:</p><p>In implementing the remediation of the historically contaminated Karosta Canal site in Liepāja, the Liepāja SEZ Authority aims to create added value for society by helping meet HELCOM’s Baltic Sea Action Plan objective of restoring the sea’s good ecological status. For additional public benefit, the Authority selected partners with expertise in marine and inland-water environmental projects and a long record of investment in scientific research. They also communicate to the public the close links between human health, the environment and the sustainable use of resources.</p><p>The public awareness campaign will be implemented by the association “Baltijas krasti”, a member of the international organisation EUCC Coastal &amp; Marine Union since 2010. Its role is to plan and implement communication and publicity activities and prepare their results. It contributes years of experience and knowledge in successful project delivery and in sharing information on environmental and climate issues with the wider public and specific target groups.</p><p>The Norwegian Institute for Water Research (NIVA) will facilitate the exchange of experience among the project partners. NIVA is Norway’s leading institute for basic and applied research on marine and fresh waters. Its research covers a wide range of environmental, climate and resource-related fields. NIVA has world-class interdisciplinary expertise and broad scientific activities, combining research, monitoring, assessment, problem-solving and consultancy services at international, national and local levels.</p><p>NIVA will demonstrate and provide examples of remediation work at comparable sites in Norway, share expertise with the Latvian personnel who will be responsible for maintaining the remediated site, and prepare practical recommendations for further measures required to remove the site from HELCOM’s hot-spot list. NIVA will contribute its long-established understanding of marine ecology, the actual interactions of pollutants in the environment and methods for correctly assessing their effects on human health and the environment. The institute will in turn gain experience of addressing the legacy of a large contaminated military site in Liepāja and understanding its actual impact on the Baltic Sea’s ecological status.</p><p>Based on its longstanding experience of implementing earlier phases of the Karosta Canal project, the Liepāja SEZ Authority has concluded that the expertise of professional partners is essential to successful delivery. Karosta Canal remediation is unique on a European scale, and its results must meet best practice in environmental protection projects. NIVA was selected specifically for its expertise in marine ecology. After remediation is complete, the Authority will need knowledge of further ecological research, monitoring and management necessary to ensure that the remediated site meets HELCOM’s Baltic Sea Action Plan objective of achieving good ecological status in the Baltic Sea.</p><p>The project implementation period is from 1 August 2021 to 31 December 2023.</p><p>The project budget is EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000.00 in co-financing from the Norwegian Financial Mechanism.</p><p> </p><p>“Working together for a green Europe”</p></div>
